1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:19,470
Welcome to the SubClub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses. We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors, and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

2
00:00:20,610 --> 00:00:32,970
SubClub is brought to you by Revenue Cat. Thousands of the world's best apps trust Revenue Cat to power in-app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

3
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:38,430
You can learn more at revenuecat.com. Let's get into the show.

4
00:00:41,610 --> 00:01:03,810
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard. My guest today is Anata Lakra, co-founder and CEO of BoldVoice, the AI speech coaching app for non-native English speakers. On the podcast, I talk with Anata about scaling to 10 million in ARR with a team of 10, having every employee do two user interviews a week, and why their

5
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:09,450
hard paywall win backfired. Hey, Anata, thanks so much for joining me on the podcast today.

6
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:11,790
Thank you for having me. I'm pumped.

7
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:20,430
Yeah, I'm really looking forward to this conversation. So you, like so many builders, built something for yourself. You

8
00:01:21,870 --> 00:01:39,750
came to the US and you know what, let me I'm about to tell your story. Let me just have you tell the story because I think it's a fantastic story and a fantastic example of how when you face real-world problems that you need solutions to, sometimes software is a great solution and that often is the best way to build something.

9
00:01:40,290 --> 00:01:52,830
Yeah, so let me go all the way back. I was born and raised in Albania. I lived there for the first 18 years of my life, and I moved to the US for college. When I moved here, I had not I had never actually been to the US before. It was my first time visiting.

10
00:01:53,790 --> 00:02:07,590
I didn't have any family or friends here, so it was a pretty big move. And I felt pretty prepared for it at the time because, you know, I applied to colleges, figured out the whole process on my own. I'd gotten into Yale, which was, you know,

11
00:02:08,850 --> 00:02:22,230
especially at the time, a huge accomplishment, and felt, you know, very confident when I first moved here. So imagine my surprise when I started to speak and people were asking me to repeat myself and were clearly not understanding me.

12
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:42,810
And it really, again, surprised me because by that point I'd learned English for 10 years and since I was basically 8. So I passed all the tests, right? I'd gotten A's and everything. So why were people not understanding me? And from that experience, I realized that you can learn a language on paper, but it's actually very different to communicate it confidently in real life.

13
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:59,070
Your pronunciation, your accent, your fluency, your conversational skills, all of those things can get in the way of being clearly understood. And I again, I realized there was this big gap and all that learning had not prepared me for that moment that I would actually communicate in a native English speaking environment.

14
00:02:59,610 --> 00:03:15,570
And it definitely played a big role at the time, both in classroom settings where I'd raise my hand and say something and people would ask me to repeat myself, or later when it came to job interviews where, again, I would have to think about I'm not actually sure how to say this word.

15
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:27,990
Let me think of a synonym on the spot because I don't want to butcher this. So I realized it's a pretty big problem. And, you know, many, many years later, I decided to start BoldVoice to help people, again, at this last mile of fluency.

16
00:03:28,050 --> 00:03:39,090
They've done all this learning on paper, but they still maybe struggle to communicate as clearly and be as confident in English as they are in their native language. And the job of BoldVoice is to fill that gap.

17
00:03:39,870 --> 00:03:46,530
Awesome. And then your co-founder kind of has a similar story. How did you meet him and what's his story?

18
00:03:47,070 --> 00:04:09,390
His story is that he actually moved to the US when his parents immigrated when he was 4. So he's essentially a native speaker, but his parents, when they moved, they were in their 30s. And even though, you know, well-educated software engineers, they still face the ceiling of having this accent and feeling like, well, I can't speak as well and as beautifully as my American

19
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:27,210
colleagues, and therefore I don't feel comfortable managing people. And they were never, like, being offered those managerial opportunities and those promotion opportunities to a great extent due to their communication confidence. So he's seen this from the perspective of his parents, which was clearly, again, very aligned with our mission.

20
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:42,090
And we actually met in both of our first jobs out of college in consulting. And I think we very clearly and quickly both realized that financial services consulting was not what we wanted to build our careers around.

21
00:04:42,150 --> 00:05:02,970
And we both wanted to actually build something, do something more entrepreneurial. And so we actually both together left that consulting company to build a startup in a very different space. But we're both now, with BoldVoice, second time found co-founders together, which I think says a lot about the co-founding relationship and that trust that you need to have with your co-founder.

22
00:05:03,270 --> 00:05:09,330
That's awesome. I didn't look deep enough in your LinkedIn. Was it McKinsey? And you just don't want to say it or was it?

23
00:05:09,510 --> 00:05:12,870
I cannot share. Cannot confirm or deny.

24
00:05:14,010 --> 00:05:27,810
You know, it's funny because I feel like there are so many stories, though. Some of those consulting companies, you know, they just have a really high bar to hire and, like, you can make fun of them and maybe they overcharge for some of their services and maybe they give obvious, you know, answers to some folks.

25
00:05:27,930 --> 00:05:43,050
But boy, I've known a lot of really sharp people who go on to be very successful founders who got their career started at McKinsey or Accenture or some of these consulting companies. So, like, I know there's a little bit of a stigma around it, but, like, honestly, like, I just know so many great people.

26
00:05:43,230 --> 00:06:04,830
No, that's very true. But actually, let me tell you this one funny story. I applied to McKinsey in college and I did not I mean, I got the interview, but I did not get an offer from McKinsey. And that was one of the cases of those interviews where I felt like, how am I going to, especially when a big part of being a consultant, right, is that clarity and the communication confidence.

27
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:21,690
I struggled to actually communicate my ideas that confidently. And I actually got an offer from a Swiss consulting firm where my interviewers were non-native speakers. So we could get way faster into the core of the ideas as opposed to, like, the accent and the communication.

28
00:06:22,290 --> 00:06:42,270
And then when I was in business school about, you know, 5, 6, however many years later, I got my offer from McKinsey because by that point I'd worked on my communication and on my accent. So it's kind of a really interesting, again, another validation point of the problem we're going after and a little bit of a funny full circle moment.

29
00:06:42,570 --> 00:06:54,090
That is so fun. I'm glad I asked because that is such a fun story and a perfect example of why you built this company. So then take me through. You were co-founders with Ilya is his name, right?

30
00:06:54,270 --> 00:06:54,450
Yeah.

31
00:06:54,810 --> 00:07:06,810
So you and Ilya founded a separate company, maybe just speed run how that went, but then, like, you know, the start of BoldVoice. Did you raise a bunch of money? Like, how did you get that first product into market? What did that look like?

32
00:07:06,990 --> 00:07:23,190
That first startup that we did was a recruiting technology company, and we were bringing more data analytics primarily through third-party references to employers so they could make better hiring decisions from the start instead of suffering the cost of hiring a person who was not the right fit.

33
00:07:23,490 --> 00:07:39,090
We were 23, 24 at the time. It was our first startup. We had barely had any experience, but it was an incredible learning experience of how to build a product, how to talk to users. And in hindsight, there were a lot of, again, the typical mistakes that first-time founders made.

34
00:07:39,090 --> 00:07:54,570
We made the same things. We started obsessing with the solution before really understanding the problem and talking to users. So I learned a lot through that experience that you can just build a product in a vacuum and then bring it to customers. You actually have to involve them from the very beginning of that process.

35
00:07:54,990 --> 00:08:07,110
And we had some traction with it. We had over a dozen companies using it, thousands of candidates going through it, but we just didn't have quite the momentum to raise a big round at the time and be able to continue with it.

36
00:08:07,290 --> 00:08:24,150
So eventually, we sunset that company. And I, through that experience, though, found my passion in product and in building the product experiences, talking to users, understanding and converting that into, like, beautiful functional UX and, again, product features that solve problems.

37
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:38,970
So with that, I went into a different Series B at the time startup in New York. Then from there, I went to build product at Peloton, which was really fun, and another kind of consumer-facing product that at the time was, you know, exploding.

38
00:08:39,210 --> 00:08:44,910
And it was a really fun experience to build product there. And then we started BoldVoice when I was

39
00:08:46,530 --> 00:09:00,990
in business school, and I was, again, thinking about, you know, having done a startup before, you realize how difficult it is. You realize that it sounds sexy from the outside, but actually the inner workings are just grinding nonstop.

40
00:09:01,110 --> 00:09:15,930
And the more you accomplish, in a way, the more the intensity ramps up. So I was very picky about exploring ideas because I knew that if I was going to do something again, it needed to be something where I could see myself spending the next decade of my life working on that problem.

41
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:32,070
And the BoldVoice idea came to me because, again, in a way, it had been in my head for a decade since I first moved here. And it was also inspired by what I was starting to read. Again, this was 2021. This was before ChatGPT and some of these latest things that we're seeing now with AI.

42
00:09:32,250 --> 00:09:44,310
But already what I've started to read more about audio AI becoming more powerful. So the idea there is, can we build something with AI that can hear you as well as a coach would?

43
00:09:44,370 --> 00:10:05,430
Because for when it comes to speech coaching, accent coaching, you can't really just give people thumbs up, thumbs down. You really need super precise feedback for it to work. So at the time, it seemed like with how audio AI was progressing, we could build those ears on the machine to actually build a product that could function like a speech coach in your pocket without needing that person in the room.

44
00:10:06,090 --> 00:10:12,750
And so that was a thesis. And, you know, now we're here five years later and we're still building around that same idea.

45
00:10:13,110 --> 00:10:22,110
Yeah. So then did you raise money? Was this a side project where you're at business school? Did Ilya quit his job? Like, what was the actual founding story?

46
00:10:22,650 --> 00:10:35,910
Yeah, so I was six months shy of graduating from Harvard Business School. I decided to say no to my McKinsey return offer so I could focus full-time on this. And I gave myself a deadline. I was graduating in six months.

47
00:10:36,150 --> 00:10:50,250
I told myself within those six months, I need a term sheet. And if I don't have a term sheet, then I can start applying to jobs. But if I do, of course, I'll continue this full-time. And in the meantime, I'm doing this 110%.

48
00:10:50,310 --> 00:11:05,010
I'm not entertaining any other projects. So that was a great that deadline was a great forcing mechanism to have that 100%, again, focus that I think is needed. Otherwise, a startup won't happen if your attention is split in a lot of different ways.

49
00:11:05,610 --> 00:11:19,230
So got to work very quickly. The first thing that I realized is I'm the user, but I'm not the expert here. I'm not the speech coach. So first thing I did was to reach out to a bunch of different speech coaches who are experts in the field.

50
00:11:19,230 --> 00:11:33,510
I had this idea that if we got Hollywood speech coaches to work with us, that it would be especially differentiated and very cool because these are people who work with celebrities and actors, and they often also have an acting background themselves.

51
00:11:33,570 --> 00:11:50,910
So they would be really great people to have involved in this project. So that was my first thing that I did. Reached out, explored conversations with them. I also, once we had our first coach, Ron, who's incredible and still works with us now five years later, put together a pilot with him where he recorded a bunch of videos.

52
00:11:51,210 --> 00:12:05,370
I found a few people, about a dozen people who wanted to, who were willing to do a beta run of this. And we were sending them manually these videos, and they were giving us their speech recordings back in emails. And the coach was giving them feedback asynchronously.

53
00:12:05,370 --> 00:12:18,690
So we're just doing the dry run of super manually without any lines of code being written just to see how this would actually go and work when we actually had a real product out there. So with all that being said, then I started to pitch investors.

54
00:12:18,930 --> 00:12:34,290
And through that process, we landed our first term sheet of around $500K. And then once we had that term sheet, we also applied around the same time to YC because we had the term sheet. We had them accelerate our interview process.

55
00:12:34,410 --> 00:12:42,330
So we had the term sheet in hand. We also got into YC. So at that point, Ilya quit his job and we're both in it full-time.

56
00:12:42,930 --> 00:12:50,250
Awesome. And so since you went through YC, you both moved to the Valley for, what, three months to go through the program?

57
00:12:50,610 --> 00:13:08,550
We would have had it not been that this is summer 2021 and COVID. So we actually built it out of New York. I have some slight regret that that was the case and we didn't get to do that. Although we do go there every few months or so to see our advisors there and to, like, a lot of our investors are based there.

58
00:13:08,610 --> 00:13:22,710
So it's good to go back. But, yeah, we didn't have that in-person experience. So in a way, we, yeah, we didn't have any distractions. We were just in a tiny office in New York just working 14-hour days to make as much progress as possible.

59
00:13:23,070 --> 00:13:26,310
When did the product actually launch? During YC?

60
00:13:26,670 --> 00:13:30,150
Yeah, we launched it in the first couple of weeks of YC.

61
00:13:30,630 --> 00:13:47,310
How did that go? I know you were charging from the very beginning, right, which was maybe a little counterintuitive compared to what, you know, a lot of venture-backed companies would do, especially at the time in 2021 when money was free and there was still a little bit of a just, like, build it, grow it fast kind of mentality.

62
00:13:48,750 --> 00:14:11,310
Yeah, the biggest thing here and the way I think about charging is that especially early in the early days, you really want to figure out who is your customer, who are you building for? Because the tool that you build, although it has a value proposition, right, that could be pretty broad, there will be an ICP that will be your initial target audience that you need to hyper-focus on and make the product better for them before you can think about multiple

63
00:14:11,550 --> 00:14:27,930
ICPs. And so charging for the product is a way to already figure out who that ICP is. And this way, you don't get feedback or worry and get distracted about feedback or usage patterns or issues with people who are just never going to be your ICP.

64
00:14:27,990 --> 00:14:41,430
But you can really figure out who has the willingness to pay. If they have the willingness to pay, that generally correlates with, okay, this problem clearly resonates with this person. Let's then narrow down and focus on making that person's experience as great as possible.

65
00:14:42,030 --> 00:14:59,970
It makes a ton of sense. Not everybody does that, but it really does make a ton of sense. How did you structure that? Was it just a standard seven-day free trial to a hard paywall? Did you have any kind of, like, free tier or any kind of free stuff that people could continue doing in the app, or was it just pay or leave the app?

66
00:15:00,330 --> 00:15:14,850
At the time, we had a very simple, I believe, $10 a month that was just the standard paywall. We always had a one-week free trial because we believe that people do want to need to experience a product before they can commit.

67
00:15:14,850 --> 00:15:33,630
We were not trying to, you know, stick them with a product and a payment that they regretted. But having, again, that free trial was still a much stronger signal than just totally ungaited. And then, yeah, we were not, especially in the YC days, we were not trying to revolutionize, you know, the business model.

68
00:15:33,810 --> 00:15:50,130
Let's just do the tried and true, just, like, one-week trial, simple $10 a month so that we can focus our energy on actually building a good product experience, building a good onboarding, building the core product instead of just having to do, like, a ton of experimentation on the paywall.

69
00:15:50,190 --> 00:16:07,050
Obviously, that came later. We've done a lot of variants on the paywall, on the pricing, packaging. And today, we actually lead with an annual subscription of around $150 a year. And then we also offer a monthly as a fallback option for people who prefer that.

70
00:16:07,050 --> 00:16:19,710
And we also have a higher tier that comes with a few additional features, like AI chat, which comes at $200 a year. So we have certainly evolved it, but I still think my philosophy is do optimize the paywall.

71
00:16:19,770 --> 00:16:35,730
It's important, but don't over-optimize the paywall compared to the actual product value that you're delivering to users, which is the onboarding, is that storytelling, and that prepping the user for a successful experience, and then obviously the core day-one-plus product experience.

72
00:16:36,270 --> 00:16:50,850
Yeah, I love that perspective. And the way you put it in the notes was optimize product value, not just the paywall and onboarding. I kind of harp on it a lot in this podcast, so I get to get up on my soapbox again because you put it so eloquently.

73
00:16:50,910 --> 00:17:08,490
But if you're spending so much time optimizing the onboarding and the paywall and you're getting a ton of trials, but you have a massive trial cancellation rate, if you think that another two pages in onboarding, another hack on your paywall is what's going to get those trial conversions, that's not how it works.

74
00:17:09,690 --> 00:17:21,990
It's because people actually get into the product and they're not finding value. Like, that's a massive optimization lever on free trial conversions that not a lot of people are really spending enough time thinking about after the paywall.

75
00:17:21,990 --> 00:17:24,990
So I love that you brought that up and that's how you think about it.

76
00:17:25,290 --> 00:17:38,730
Yeah, I mean, look, all these hacks of, like, oh, just one copy change and it drives 20% more people. Like, they're so tempting because you're like, oh, I'm just one iteration away from unlocking this huge product value.

77
00:17:38,850 --> 00:17:51,510
And of course, again, I'm not saying do no testing at all. It is an important product surface area, but I think people have taken it maybe to an extreme where they're putting so much effort in that as opposed to, again, working on the core product experience.

78
00:17:51,810 --> 00:18:04,350
Another really interesting thing that we found in some of those experiments is that the initial metrics actually look really promising. For example, the start trial rate does increase with a few changes that you might make. And I can give specific examples if that's helpful.

79
00:18:04,410 --> 00:18:24,510
But at the end of the day, when you actually look who converted, did those payments go through or did they fail? Did people ask for refunds? The delta after all that work with the bottom line is actually, we found for a lot of these experiments, not that big, if not sometimes even negative, even though the headline number of trial start rate is higher.

80
00:18:25,170 --> 00:18:38,130
So I've kind of gotten a little, yeah, disillusioned with these, like, again, almost get-rich-quick-quick schemes that feels like paywall optimization is becoming. And it's like at the end of the day, just make a better product for people.

81
00:18:38,190 --> 00:18:40,170
And that's how you drive more revenue.

82
00:18:40,590 --> 00:19:02,430
Yeah. And to be clear, I mean, some of those hacks work and you're experimenting with them. You try stuff. But I love, and I'm just going to read it from my notes because I love the way you frame this, is that you're the ultimate judge, the ultimate metric you should be measuring against is not trial starts and some of those early funnel, not even, like, initial conversions and stuff, but it's net revenue after refunds per exposed user.

83
00:19:02,850 --> 00:19:13,230
And then here's the kicker, on a matured cohort. How did you decide on that specifically as your kind of gold standard metric to measure any experiment against?

84
00:19:13,590 --> 00:19:26,670
It came from the experience of running a lot of these and implementing some changes that looked really good. And in hindsight, again, at that time, maybe we just didn't know that we had to wait and see all these other metrics mature.

85
00:19:26,730 --> 00:19:42,450
And also, we didn't have instrumentation. It was really easy day one to see start trial rate, right? And that's where you get the most data. That's where, you know, you're most likely to get a p-value that is statistically significant because you get so many people at that level.

86
00:19:42,930 --> 00:19:58,890
But what we learned and started to implement soon after we started to really iterate on the paywall is that's not what actually matters. You have to actually look at the metrics of actual subscription conversion, which, again, that's a pretty well-known thing.

87
00:19:58,950 --> 00:20:19,110
I don't think that's new. But the more important thing there is that some of those subscriptions, even though initially they convert, the payment doesn't actually go through. And a lot of the refund rate can be a huge delta. So what I found is that anything that kind of forces or tricks people into starting a trial, you'll pay it when it comes to refunds.

88
00:20:19,770 --> 00:20:33,030
And that is a worse experience because those people who ask for refunds are generally pissed. They're not happy that they have to go through that process. You're also creating this negative user sentiment around the product that you don't want.

89
00:20:33,390 --> 00:20:55,470
And then, of course, the matured cohort piece is important because people won't ask for the refund necessarily on day one. They'll ask for the refund after they've actually seen that the payment hit their bank account. So you have to think about, okay, trial start on this date, give it an extra week for them to convert, give it at least two extra weeks for the dust to settle from refunds.

90
00:20:55,890 --> 00:21:02,850
So actually, again, it's very tempting to make a quick decision on trials, but you have to give it more time to see the entire story play out.

91
00:21:03,150 --> 00:21:21,750
Yeah, it makes so much sense, but it's hard to do and it's hard to find the discipline. And then, like you were saying, the tooling, I mean, RevenueCat, we just, you know, in the last year or two, finally expanded the experimentation window where you can actually go back and look at three months and six months.

92
00:21:21,810 --> 00:21:37,710
And I think we now, it's like over a year that you can look back. And, you know, we've tried to make that really easy because it's one of those things, like, in if you're running your own analytics, like, it's just hard to do that and then have the discipline to go back and check it and all that kind of stuff.

93
00:21:38,070 --> 00:21:55,410
It's all these things that make sense, but then you actually have to execute. You actually have to do it and then have the discipline to keep looking back and maybe reversing some of those decisions that you were really excited about. Like, I mean, because I would imagine you do sometimes pick a winner on some of those early metrics.

94
00:21:55,710 --> 00:22:03,810
Then you don't see till three months later that it's something you actually wanted to reverse. Has that happened frequently or how does that work for you?

95
00:22:04,170 --> 00:22:18,690
Yeah, I don't think we've implemented a decision that we've had to reverse. But, yeah, there have been many things where we've been really excited about the initial result. And the hardest thing is not necessarily the discipline and checking back on the data.

96
00:22:18,750 --> 00:22:36,630
It's the fact that it takes just way longer. And you have data you're excited about, you want to be able to action it. And the idea of just sitting there on something that could be really promising for an extra month or depending on X number of months, depending on how much, again, users you need to validate and get to statistical significance.

97
00:22:36,810 --> 00:22:51,150
That part is hard of, like, just sitting on that and being patient. And it also means you can iterate as fast, right? You can implement those learnings as fast. So I get it. And I was that impatient person. What we found is, for example, I'll give you two examples, actually.

98
00:22:51,270 --> 00:23:13,350
When it comes to the paywall, we tested a hard paywall where we basically removed the X dismiss button. So people had to start a trial in order to proceed. And then we also tested a version where there was still a dismiss button on the main paywall, but then if you dismissed it, we gave people an offer of X percent off that

99
00:23:13,530 --> 00:23:28,950
they could not X out of. So they would have to start a trial. If they hadn't started at the baseline price, they would have to start it at the lower price. Again, it's a trial. It's not a payment. So they still can cancel it. But both of those look so promising on the trial numbers.

100
00:23:29,190 --> 00:23:41,370
And it makes sense, right? People have already put in all that time in the onboarding flow. They actually want to see what this app is all about. And we're like, great, this is great. Why are we sitting on this? Why shouldn't just we put this in front of everyone?

101
00:23:41,730 --> 00:23:56,370
And the answer came down to the refunds. And the refunds spiked by way more than the gain in start trials. And again, it took us a while to get to that data. But once the data came in, I think it was very clear that that was not the right thing to do.

102
00:23:56,790 --> 00:24:10,230
So again, I feel like I've been a broken record already about this. But I think my takeaway from this is that just the mechanical clever things don't actually work and you'll pay the price for those things.

103
00:24:10,290 --> 00:24:16,890
And the only thing that actually works is once you have something that generally makes sense when it comes to the paywall, just work on the product.

104
00:24:17,730 --> 00:24:32,490
So how does it work now? You said you tested a fully hard paywall and then you tested the X to a fully hard paywall with a discount. What happens now if they X out of the paywall? Because you said that you don't really have a freemium experience, right?

105
00:24:32,790 --> 00:24:46,410
We have a very limited freemium experience. So if you X out of the paywall, you're taken to the speech assessment. You'll be able to speak. You'll still see the assessment results will break down all the things that we heard. It's already a pretty valuable experience.

106
00:24:46,530 --> 00:25:02,490
Like some people work with coaches to just get that assessment. So we give that for free. And then you can also access the rest of the day one experience and do a practice set and I believe also coach video for free. And then only on day two, you can start a trial at that point if you wish to continue.

107
00:25:03,030 --> 00:25:20,910
So we've kind of, yeah, found the middle ground and let's give some of the product experience, but we also have an experience where we have daily plans. So it's easy to say, okay, the first daily plan for free on us. After that, again, you don't have to pay right away, but you have to start a seven-day trial to continue.

108
00:25:21,210 --> 00:25:34,050
It's essentially a reverse trial at that point because you are, like, giving value, but only for that first set of lessons and the speech assessment. These are the kind of things I think more people should be experimenting with.

109
00:25:34,590 --> 00:25:47,730
Is there something, is there a great experience I can give to folks that helps them see the value of the product, but that there is some kind of limit to it? And a great example, I just brought this up on a live stream yesterday.

110
00:25:48,030 --> 00:26:02,190
I've been looking at trading card games apps a lot in the past few weeks because we're going to do a live app evaluation on SubClub of one of these trading card game apps. And it's like a card scanner to see the value of your Pokémon cards and Magic cards and things like that.

111
00:26:02,730 --> 00:26:15,690
And one of the really cool things I did, and I hadn't seen this exact user experience before, but what they do is they give essentially the full value of the product, but then they limit it to a certain number of scans and then they make it really clear.

112
00:26:15,930 --> 00:26:28,350
So you've got your whole scanner UI where you're scanning your Pokémon card and it says, like, you know, 35 scans left and then you scan one and then 34 scans left and then you scan one, 33 scans left.

113
00:26:28,470 --> 00:26:41,490
And so it's really prepping you for that moment that the paywall comes, but it's giving you the full value of the product and a really fantastic experience generally. I mean, some of them are really bad at scanning the cards, as I've learned.

114
00:26:42,150 --> 00:27:04,230
My kids are into both Magic and Pokémon and they've been pulling me in recently. So I've been actually using them with their card collections and I've collected a few now. So some of them actually are bad experience. And this is kind of your point, though, is like those apps that are a fantastic experience, it's way better for me to have that experience and then use the product and then I'm going to be a product user for life.

115
00:27:04,290 --> 00:27:26,790
I'm going to be, you know, actually subscribe versus if I'd have done a seven-day free trial, it looks really great. I started the trial, but then I immediately canceled because the product's not delivering. So if you can actually deliver value and people actually care, that kind of finding a way to do a reverse trial, finding a way to do some kind of limited experience, trying to just find ways to, like, give value really

116
00:27:27,690 --> 00:27:33,690
preps the customer for that next paywall that they're going to be way more likely to convert.

117
00:27:33,990 --> 00:27:47,910
And the cool thing is that at least for us, you don't need to do that at the expense of that paywall and onboarding. There will be some people who through the onboarding experience have seen enough about the product that they're prepped to start a free seven-day trial.

118
00:27:48,390 --> 00:28:01,530
There will be some people who are more skeptical and maybe they have been burned in the past by free trials or other apps that have shady product experiences and they even just putting their credit card down, they don't want to do it, even if it's for a free experience.

119
00:28:01,950 --> 00:28:17,490
So for those people, you can offer that. I think offering for everyone and just removing the free trial paywall from the experience, I think that could be tricky because you can also maybe risk that people think, oh, this is just a free product and then get kind of upset when you show them a paywall.

120
00:28:17,490 --> 00:28:29,670
So I think we've kind of found an interesting, nice middle ground where we do show them that paywall, they can X out of it and they can get a little bit of a free taster before they commit to putting their credit card down for that free experience.

121
00:28:30,030 --> 00:28:48,090
Do you think you'll ever have a more kind of freemium tier where people can get some value and continue using the app? Or like, what's your philosophy on that? Because I know when we were prepping for the episode, you mentioned that you really enjoyed the Jim Cantu, the Chief Product Officer at Duolingo, his episode.

122
00:28:48,150 --> 00:29:03,990
And like, their big thesis at Duolingo is that that freemium base is the growth engine that they lever. What's your philosophy on that? Do you ever see freemium or do you think that this form of kind of reverse trial and giving away value is enough to keep propelling the business forward?

123
00:29:04,590 --> 00:29:24,030
Jim is great. I'm a big fan of him and the work he's done at Duolingo. I will say, though, that it depends on the product and it depends on the business model. And I think the worst thing that someone can do is, like, take some playbooks and some elements of a playbook from one app, combine it with a playbook of a different app and create this mishmash strategy.

124
00:29:24,210 --> 00:29:37,230
Duolingo's strategy has always been huge freemium base, right, that they monetize a portion of. And because they have this, like, all the languages, all the levels, they're teaching the basics of everything. They now have all these new skills.

125
00:29:37,530 --> 00:29:50,190
Give this product away for free. Even if you monetize a percentage of it, you're fine. Our philosophy from the beginning is we're not going after this, like, huge base of learners who are at basic level. We are actually intentionally ignoring them.

126
00:29:50,250 --> 00:30:03,270
We're not teaching the basics of English. We're not teaching words. We're focusing on a very different audience that is actually somewhat ignored or deprioritized by a lot of these apps, which is people who already have a pretty good command of language.

127
00:30:03,330 --> 00:30:15,570
I want to take it to the next level. So people are at intermediate to advanced level and for whom English is important enough that they want to put more effort behind it. And those people are generally career professionals. They care more about English.

128
00:30:15,630 --> 00:30:37,470
They're willing to pay. The alternative to what we offer is a way more expensive one-on-one speech coaching experience with an English teacher and accent coach. So it's a totally different audience where the intent is much higher and the ROI is also way higher than as a hobby learning the basics of Spanish or Japanese or something like that.

129
00:30:37,830 --> 00:30:56,730
So totally different ICP, different value proposition, different willingness to pay. And so our entire business has been optimized around that. And it makes sense that, again, for Duolingo, if they do way too heavy monetization at the sacrifice of their free base, that's the free base is what got them here.

130
00:30:56,790 --> 00:31:14,850
So that doesn't make sense. Whereas for us, that was never the case. We're always hyper-focused on this person who has a willingness to pay if they get a product that solves that need. And if obviously the cost of that product is a significant reduction to working one-on-one with a coach, which is the case for us.

131
00:31:15,450 --> 00:31:29,730
As you say that, though, it does make me think that at some point, you know, as you grow and have, you know, more time and resources to experiment with, that maybe there is a cheaper tier that is aimed at the more casual, but still willing to pay.

132
00:31:29,910 --> 00:31:42,330
And again, I think this is something if you're building an app and you start to see, because I imagine this wasn't, like, from the get-go, you realized, oh, we're going to sell this to professionals who are willing to pay hundreds of dollars for this.

133
00:31:42,690 --> 00:31:55,890
So if you start seeing yourself pulled in that direction, like, embrace it because it can, like, you know, $150 a year and then a premium tier above that that's $200. And honestly, like, you're probably just scratching the surface of what you could do.

134
00:31:55,890 --> 00:32:17,970
And then you mentioned you even have a one-on-one coaching that, you know, it's not a huge part of the business, but it's like $400, $500 an hour. So there is, like, massive willingness to pay when it's on that side of things. And so by not chasing that consumer and lower willingness to pay market, you're building a really fantastic business on that higher ROI, higher intent, more disposable income, and all those kind of things.

135
00:32:18,390 --> 00:32:36,570
Yeah. And that's served us well till this point. And I'm not going to say we'll never deviate from that strategy. I think for sure there will be experimentations with the packaging, especially as we go right now, we're still primarily serving people in the US who are immigrants and who also have that higher ability to pay.

136
00:32:36,690 --> 00:32:49,770
Whereas outside of the US, spoken English fluency is sometimes as important, right? Because you can get a higher paid job, you can get better opportunities, you can go to study at a different international university and so forth.

137
00:32:49,830 --> 00:33:02,130
It unlocks even, in some cases, a bigger delta between the opportunities you could get without that clear English and those you can get with clear communication. But of course, the base ability to pay in general is lower.

138
00:33:02,190 --> 00:33:24,330
And I think as we do more of the international strategy, we certainly can revisit and should revisit, like, the localization and things like that. So I'm not saying this is a strategy necessarily that we will rigidly keep as we continue to scale, but certainly the principle that we're serving a different learner level where they are higher intent

139
00:33:24,750 --> 00:33:32,070
and willing to pay for a product that solves their need instead of just like a hobby learner, I think that will most likely continue to be the case.

140
00:33:32,490 --> 00:33:34,710
How did you figure out that that was your market?

141
00:33:35,010 --> 00:33:49,650
Well, in the early days we created this product, I went back to the basics and I said, well, I was an international student when I first experienced this problem. And it's like such a almost a cliché playbook, right? Let's just put this in colleges because college students are great adopters.

142
00:33:49,770 --> 00:34:04,170
And again, this is what happens going back to my point about, like, you take a playbook from a totally different product, like dating app or something, where they all start in college campuses or Facebook famously or whatever. And you apply it to products where the ICP and the value prop is super different.

143
00:34:04,170 --> 00:34:24,390
And of course, it's not going to work. So what we found in that process is that it's not like international students were a terrible audience for us. I mean, we still do get a lot of students in the app, but we clearly realized that, A, they are a bit younger and they have more, they still have some plasticity and they're still surrounded by native speakers in very social environments.

144
00:34:24,510 --> 00:34:37,470
So their rate of improvement naturally will be higher without needing this additional resource if they just focus on it themselves. The second thing is the willingness to pay and the ability to pay for college students is, of course, lower.

145
00:34:37,530 --> 00:35:00,210
And also just the fact that they maybe have not seen the price of their communication as much because they're not yet in a meeting where people will overlook them and listen to the native speaker over them, or they haven't yet been in a job interview where they realized that the reason that that didn't work out was their communication and all

146
00:35:00,270 --> 00:35:19,050
of those things. So both the willingness to pay and the realization of how much it impacts your income and your career advancement has not happened yet. So we found that actually the audience that resonates the best with this were people who were in the workplace already and who were already seeing the cost and therefore the ROI of investing their career.

147
00:35:19,050 --> 00:35:23,430
And of course, they were making, having actually income that they could put towards this.

148
00:35:23,850 --> 00:35:42,990
And then is there, has there been any kind of more direct B2B play with companies with, and because I did see on Hacker News, the founder of Expo said that they talked about having an employee expense this as a solution to help them improve their job performance.

149
00:35:43,290 --> 00:35:56,310
Yeah, we've seen that quite a bit with just inbound companies will reach to us to make this happen for their employees. Or we're also seeing that people will, you know, buy this and then have the company pay for it, which is great.

150
00:35:56,610 --> 00:36:02,730
We haven't pursued it as much as an intentional go-to-market purely because of resourcing. We're still

151
00:36:04,290 --> 00:36:17,490
a very small team and we've gotten to this point by being relentlessly focused on the consumer experience. And I think the muscle of building a B2B company or B2B go-to-market is pretty different.

152
00:36:17,550 --> 00:36:35,010
And I think it'll make sense for us at a future stage potentially. But for now, the consumer market is so big that we've just let it be our main intentional focus and then we get this nice additional effect of inbound from companies because they know that the consumer product is so loved.

153
00:36:35,190 --> 00:36:45,030
Yeah. One of the things you touched on there, and we've kind of been touching on throughout the entire conversation, is user obsession and focus. I love your approach to

154
00:36:46,350 --> 00:36:57,570
understanding users. So why don't you take me through kind of the layers of how you speak to your users, how you interact with users, and how you let that impact the direction of the product.

155
00:36:58,050 --> 00:37:11,550
So we've put a bunch of things in place to make the user obsession not just something we talk about, but like a muscle that we keep fresh because I think the user base is always also going to change and their expectations are going to change.

156
00:37:11,910 --> 00:37:33,150
You're going to bring in naturally newer people as you expand. So it's really important to not just have this do a bunch of user interviews at the beginning and then be like, okay, that's our user and then just like not evolve that understanding. So the things that we've implemented is, for example, we send out automated invites to users and we pick a variety of different people.

157
00:37:33,330 --> 00:37:49,770
Sometimes it's subscribers who have been with us for two or three years, sometimes people who just started trial a week ago and converted or didn't convert. And we ask them to jump on a call with our team for 15 minutes and share their feedback. And that invite automatically invites the people on the team who have availability in their calendar.

158
00:37:49,890 --> 00:38:05,970
So what that creates is that basically every single person on the team, myself, my co-founder, our engineers, our growth people, everyone is in at least two user interviews a week. So we're actively talking to people for at least 15 minutes, like twice a week.

159
00:38:06,690 --> 00:38:21,690
And that's just one example. Another example is we've created this super user group and we hold weekly or so calls with them. And that's a great opportunity for this is the people who already engage with the app a lot to figure out how do we make these experiences better for them.

160
00:38:21,810 --> 00:38:40,770
And where that's been really impactful is to actually show, to have our engineers demo new features that we're working on and get feedback from those users on those features so that they're a part of the product evolution and decision-making before the feature is even out to users.

161
00:38:41,070 --> 00:38:53,190
So that's been really powerful to figure out what is making sense, what's not making sense. One thing about our product is that it's deceptively very complicated when it comes to pronunciation, speech.

162
00:38:53,250 --> 00:39:14,190
It's not like teaching grammar where there are correct and incorrect rules. There's just so many elements and details and exceptions and nuances, and it can get really complicated to build, which is what we want, like a really user-friendly product experience while also not making it overcomplicated and intimidating to use and understand.

163
00:39:14,310 --> 00:39:30,990
So it's really important to get our real users' eyes on features to validate, is this actually making sense before we ship it? So those are just two quick examples, but sometimes we also bring people in office and we watch how they actually use the product and we watch what they tap, like what they're spending most time on.

164
00:39:31,050 --> 00:39:45,270
Of course, we also have analytics around all of this that we also watch, but being able to actually verbally talk to users and actually see them interact with the product, I think, is an underrated skill.

165
00:39:45,390 --> 00:39:55,590
So building these, again, automated ways for us, semi-automated ways, of course, then we actually are in these calls, but semi-automated ways to have more user face time has been super helpful.

166
00:39:56,010 --> 00:40:10,350
I love that level of customer obsession. And it speaks to being such a small team. And we'll get to this later. You're only 10 people and at $10 million in ARR, which is incredible. A million dollars per employee as a small scrappy company is incredible.

167
00:40:10,530 --> 00:40:22,530
But that user obsession, you know, you're meeting with the users two a week, everybody at the company. So that's 20 calls a week at least. You have a super user group and then you bring people into the office.

168
00:40:22,650 --> 00:40:43,110
There's like even layers to it, but that qualitative feedback and seeing people actually struggle is so different than the quantitative, like just look at the analytics. And then when you see the analytics and you're like, oh, it looks like something's going on here, then you actually can like verify that with the super user group or with these in-office testing and those kind of things.

169
00:40:43,170 --> 00:40:55,530
So I love that like kind of multi-layer approach. And then you're also like not over-indexing on the super users because you're having meetings with the random folks and then you're actually having people in-office to get like the human touch.

170
00:40:55,650 --> 00:40:57,810
So I absolutely love how

171
00:40:59,130 --> 00:41:15,270
you've built that and structured it to kind of touch all those different points and not kind of, because, you know, it is a bit of a trap sometimes where if you only did the super user group, you'd probably be pushing the product in directions that works for them, but not some of the more casual users.

172
00:41:15,630 --> 00:41:32,490
And then if you only did the kind of casual user sampling, you may miss some of the things that the super users care about. And so by layering it all like that, you're really getting the kind of breadth of qualitative feedback that you just never would get in analytics and never would get if you didn't do it in this multi-layer strategy.

173
00:41:32,850 --> 00:41:36,990
Did that just come about organically or how did you decide on that approach?

174
00:41:37,170 --> 00:41:59,850
I mean, it's evolved over the years for sure. And more recently, again, we have been figuring out ways to build more and more automation. All of us here in the company use Claude a lot. So we're figuring out how do we do these things so that we don't have to, because if you have to have a person email manually, you know, a bunch of users, then that's great one-off, but it's a one-off.

175
00:41:59,970 --> 00:42:22,110
And so these automations help us, again, make sure that we maintain that muscle instead of just making it a one-off effort. But I will say though, to your point about the diversity of perspectives, one really important flag is that you're always going to get more feedback and you're going to get more people to jump on calls who are happy users because they're like, oh my gosh, my favorite product reached out to

176
00:42:22,230 --> 00:42:36,630
me for my feedback. I want to book this call. And people who did not have a good experience are going to be less likely to jump on those calls. So it's really important to keep that in mind and to be aware of the bias in the feedback, in the features that people are also asking for.

177
00:42:37,350 --> 00:42:55,170
A lot of the time, people who did not have a good experience or did not feel like the product met what they were looking for, those people might not want to jump on a call. But if we give them just like a three-question mini survey in an email where they don't even have to click on anything, they can just like respond in an email, they'll be more likely to answer that.

178
00:42:55,230 --> 00:43:15,510
Or when someone goes to cancel their subscription or their trial, having like a mini flow there that asks them what actually went wrong. So yeah, I think just being aware of that bias and making sure that you're not overly indexing on like making those happy people happier, which is important, but that could also lead you toward like making this like super niche, super technical product.

179
00:43:15,870 --> 00:43:21,630
And you also want to create a product that, you know, has, especially as it grows, wider and wider appeal.

180
00:43:22,410 --> 00:43:28,650
Such great advice. Gosh, there's so many things I want to talk about. We don't have a ton of time left, but maybe we can kind of speed run some of the

181
00:43:29,910 --> 00:43:44,370
virality and kind of growth things y'all have done because you've gotten some really cool growth wins and some viral moments and things like that. What would you say were the kind of maybe top three most impactful lessons on the growth side or top three kind of viral moments?

182
00:43:44,550 --> 00:44:01,890
Well, for sure, the top one was the XN Oracle that we built. So yeah, our team had this idea that if we could build a tool that could predict where a person was from just based on 10 seconds of them speaking, it could have this kind of viral potential.

183
00:44:02,010 --> 00:44:19,290
And so we built and trained a model in-house to do exactly that. So I think this also speaks to, again, a really important part of our success is that we have an AudemyL team in-house building these and training these really differentiated models from scratch with our own data.

184
00:44:19,350 --> 00:44:34,410
And we also have the expert speech coaches who are a huge part of how we label, transcribe, and just train these models to be better. So that's a really big differentiation in our product. And the XN Oracle was built based on one of those in-house created models.

185
00:44:34,890 --> 00:44:47,910
And it worked. It was the most magical thing I think we've done. And the best part about it is that the way that it spread is that every month I write the investor updates.

186
00:44:47,970 --> 00:45:00,090
I've been doing it for five years. That month that the XN Oracle was built, I sent it out, I think even the day after or so to our investors. One of them was an angel investor. It was Korean. And he was like, oh, this is cool.

187
00:45:00,150 --> 00:45:14,610
I'll put it on Threads. And he posted it on Threads. And then I believe the next day we had 100,000 people in Korea try this thing. It's again, wild and unlike anything we've seen before. So of course, when that happened, we're like, let's make this better.

188
00:45:14,730 --> 00:45:28,470
Let's invest a lot of time in making sure that making the model more accurate, making sure it doesn't crash and all these things. And it was a really wonderful way to get people familiar with the product. And we made it like there's no, there was no gate, there was no free trial.

189
00:45:28,530 --> 00:45:42,570
It was like a pretty magical experience. You don't even have to download the app, by the way. My co-founder actually made this decision to just put this in a separate web app where you just go to xnoracle.com, you record your voice, instantly spits out your feedback, your results.

190
00:45:42,630 --> 00:45:59,370
So it was optimized for this frictionless viral moment that then exposes people to Bold Voice as a brand and then invites them into the app to continue discovering more things about their speech and then actually improving and working on their pronunciation and speech as well.

191
00:45:59,910 --> 00:46:06,150
Has that been fairly evergreen or have you taken lessons from that in your ads and other viral content?

192
00:46:06,330 --> 00:46:19,230
For sure. Yeah, it's actually surprised us how much it actually has lasting appeal. There are people who use it, who keep coming back. It will randomly go viral. Again, with viral things, what makes them beautiful is also what makes them tricky.

193
00:46:20,010 --> 00:46:39,330
You can't over-engineer them like with ads where you know exactly I'm spending this much, I'm getting this much out of it. But one thing that has proven true is that when we invest into it, so for example, we recently added this really cool feature where it doesn't just tell you the country where your accent is from, but it also goes down to the city level.

194
00:46:40,050 --> 00:47:00,990
And once we implemented that, it had another viral boost because again, had this new unique edge to it. So yeah, we can constantly see it have this evergreen value with like kind of random unpredictable spikes, but we have a few levers where we can boost it by adding new features.

195
00:47:01,110 --> 00:47:20,430
Or another thing that we did is that we showed people a snippet of the sample of the full results that they would get in the app. So they're not just seeing this result and then they have to make the leap of faith to go into the app. We're telling them, by the way, here are some of the other things you'll see on the app and that invites them to get to the app or it boosts the conversion rate to get them to the app.

196
00:47:20,730 --> 00:47:25,890
Yeah, that is so cool. What's the number two top growth hack, lesson, viral moment?

197
00:47:26,070 --> 00:47:41,190
Probably when it comes to viral things, the second thing that we did, which is again, kind of funny and unpredictable, it's just like social media and TikTok. There was this trend that was going around with people crying on TikTok when the app gave them low scores.

198
00:47:41,250 --> 00:47:43,410
And in particular, there was this one creator who

199
00:47:44,850 --> 00:48:04,650
got, I don't know, she got like 300 million views in like a few months or something pretty crazy like that. Again, completely not boosted by ads. So again, another really cool way of getting the product in a more lighthearted, accessible way into the kind of people's feeds.

200
00:48:04,770 --> 00:48:27,390
So we're not just doing like this somewhat serious marketing, but we also have these other lighter ways to get in front of people and make them aware of Bold Voice. Of course, when you do humorous content, you're going to get huge viral spikes, but they're not going to have the same level of intent that you can get from like, you know, paid ads, for example, or word of mouth, obviously, where you can really target the messaging,

201
00:48:27,510 --> 00:48:32,970
but it's still a really nice, I think, machine to have working alongside the paid marketing efforts.

202
00:48:33,390 --> 00:48:43,710
Well, for number three then, I want to ask, what is your kind of bread and butter growth tactic? Do you rely on paid ads? What's the kind of the bulk of what you put your growth effort into?

203
00:48:43,890 --> 00:48:56,970
I think it's a mix for sure of product-led viral or shareable features plus paid marketing. On the paid marketing side, I know it's very vilified and a lot of people are against it. And I think that's taken to an extreme.

204
00:48:57,510 --> 00:49:10,410
And realistically, even those people who are seeing ads are better actually running ads. My experience with it is that there's a good way to run ads and a bad way to run ads. The good way to do it, I think, is to just be fully aware of your unit economics.

205
00:49:10,830 --> 00:49:23,670
So you're not spending money on, again, low value customers when it comes to, again, LTV over CAC. So there's quite a bit of discipline there that needs to happen to make sure that the unit economics are strong.

206
00:49:23,790 --> 00:49:37,830
And one really powerful thing that I have found in that process is using payback period to your advantage and making that as short as possible because it gives you upfront a lot of clarity on a user's LTV.

207
00:49:37,950 --> 00:49:53,610
And what I mean by that is when I already referred to this before with having most users by default being presented with the annual plan as opposed to the monthly plan. What that means is that with an annual plan, we get the cash upfront from users after their seven-day free trial.

208
00:49:53,910 --> 00:50:12,810
So we know right away from a particular channel, it drove this many subscribers, this is how much we spent, and then we know exactly how effective that marketing was. As opposed to monthly, where you get the initial monthly subscriber, but you don't actually know, will they retain one month or will they retain 24 months?

209
00:50:13,170 --> 00:50:22,170
And different channels actually do produce very different durations by customers, especially TikTok. I found to be a flimsier channel in terms of just

210
00:50:23,430 --> 00:50:35,430
intent and loyalty or attention span, whatever you want to call it. So it's really helpful to have annual as a way to add that discipline where upfront you know what your LTV over CAC is at least for the first year.

211
00:50:35,550 --> 00:50:44,430
